  "articleBody": "When you're trying to make your laundry room more accessible, it's easy to fall into some common traps. But don't worry, we can totally avoid them! First off, one big mistake is crowding the workspace. You need enough clear space, ideally around 30 x 48 inches in front of machines, and about 60 inches for turning, so make sure you're not trying to cram too much in. Another frequent miss is placing storage too high. Shelves and cabinets should be within an easy reach range for everyone, whether standing or seated. And if you're still using a top-loading machine, or if its controls are hard to get to, that's definitely something to reconsider for better accessibility. Don't forget about door clearance either; even if the room itself is good, a narrow doorway can still be a barrier. Lastly, choosing flooring with a lot of glare or a high slip risk is a no-go. You want something safe, non-slip, and easy to clean. Avoiding these common errors can make a huge difference in creating a truly functional and accessible laundry space. If you're unsure about plumbing or electrical, that's when a pro's insight really comes in handy.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
